\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\title{Sparse optimizer plasticity in RLVR}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{optimization}
\tag{post-training}
\tag{plasticity}

\p{The source question is whether reinforcement-learning updates need the
momentum and per-parameter adaptive rates supplied by AdamW. Mukherjee et al.
compare SGD, SGD with momentum, RMSProp, and AdamW in GRPO and PPO settings
across mathematical, coding, and adaptive-verifiable environments
(\citet{Sections 4--6 and conclusion}{mukherjee2026adam}). In the reported
runs, SGD matches or often exceeds AdamW, while its updates are substantially
sparser and lower-rank; the source reports fewer than #{0.02\%} changed
parameters in representative cases and a 15.7 GB peak-memory reduction for
Qwen3-1.7B. These are observations about the stated models, data, precision,
threshold, seeds, and budgets.}

\p{The relevant FTIP intervention is therefore a tuple, not an optimizer name:
model and checkpoint, RL algorithm, prompt and environment laws, optimizer and
hyperparameters, numerical precision, update-support rule, effective-rank rule,
training and evaluation budgets, and the held-out capability interface. A
reported reward or pass rate does not identify the update geometry that
produced it.}

\card{Definition}{optimizer update support and effective rank}{
Let #{\theta_0} and #{\theta_1} be the parameters before and after a declared
finite intervention, with #{n} trainable scalar parameters. For threshold
#{\varepsilon>0}, define
##{
 S_{\varepsilon}(\theta_0,\theta_1)=
 \frac{1}{n}\#\{i:|\theta_{1,i}-\theta_{0,i}|>\varepsilon\},
}
\p{and, for every two-dimensional weight update #{\Delta W}, let
#{\operatorname{rank}_{.99}(\Delta W)} be the least number of singular
values whose squared sum reaches 99 percent of the total squared singular-value
sum. A study must report the threshold, precision, tensor selection, and
aggregation rule alongside these numbers. The source uses bf16 and a
#{10^{-5}} threshold for its sparsity calculation; that convention is a source
field, not a universal definition.}
}

\p{A matched Phase B comparison holds model family, checkpoint, training data
and order, token and environment steps, rollout limits, precision, gradient
accumulation, seeds, evaluation prompts, and stopping rule fixed while changing
SGD or AdamW. It records optimizer-state memory, wall-clock and energy, update
support, effective rank, verifier/evaluator cost, and a prespecified held-out
transfer task. Capability change and support change are reported as separate
outcomes; a lower-rank update is not itself an acquisition witness.}

\p{The source does not establish general SGD superiority, a universal low-rank
plasticity law, or transfer to long-horizon agents. Deep Delta's learned
read/compare/write mechanism changes a different coordinate and remains in the
later recurrence/rewrite batch.}
